{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546851383,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE2ODY3OTA2MA=="},"target":"de00c2469e4baf99a70ddc13a440ce4287dad76c7cbefb5c850c22caaa7eceac","message":"I want to implement support for populating a minimal set of metadata fields from a dataset's individual metadata extract (which is in whatever format). The idea is to not have an automated mechanism report random blobs, but have a clear and tailored mapping in a dataset's configuration that defines which native metadata field to use to populate a specific field in the standard set.\n\nMy idea of a minimal set was written down (a long time ago) here:\nhttp://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/metadata.html#rfc822-compliant-metadata-datalad-rfc822\n\nToday I would probably add some kind of \"tag\" list.\n\nI personally see the target audience to be humans (pretty much a necessity, when no defined terms for the metadata values (not keys) are used).\n\nMoreover, I see this as an entirely optional feature. It won't solve the data discovery problem to any reasonable extent, but I acknowledge that I can hide the severity of the problem to some degree.\n\nPlease comment on the particular selection of metadata fields for this minimal set. Please also comment on the desired structure of the values. I personally think that anything beyond free-form text is useless, as it won't be significantly more useful (compared to actual linked data), but substantially increases the burden on users of this functionality. Hence I'd put \"must be a human-readable string\" a instructions.\n\nPS: I should clarify that such a mapping from native to common fields would be defined in the dataset that is being aggregated _into_ to avoid the need to modified subdatasets for this purpose (and for a particular purpose that may or may not be what the main function of any dataset is envisioned to be).","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546851393,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE2ODY3OTEwNA=="},"target":"de00c2469e4baf99a70ddc13a440ce4287dad76c7cbefb5c850c22caaa7eceac","message":"I want to implement support for populating a minimal set of metadata fields from a dataset's individual metadata extract (which is in whatever format). The idea is to not have an automated mechanism report random blobs, but have a clear and tailored mapping in a dataset's configuration that defines which native metadata field to use to populate a specific field in the standard set.\n\nMy idea of a minimal set was written down (a long time ago) here:\nhttp://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/metadata.html#rfc822-compliant-metadata-datalad-rfc822\n\nToday I would probably add some kind of \"tag\" list.\n\nI personally see the target audience to be humans (pretty much a necessity, when no defined terms for the metadata values (not keys) are used).\n\nMoreover, I see this as an entirely optional feature. It won't solve the data discovery problem to any reasonable extent, but I acknowledge that I can hide the severity of the problem to some degree.\n\nPlease comment on the particular selection of metadata fields for this minimal set. Please also comment on the desired structure of the values. I personally think that anything beyond free-form text is useless, as it won't be significantly more useful (compared to actual linked data), but substantially increases the burden on users of this functionality. Hence I'd put \"must be a human-readable string\" a instructions.\n\nPS: I should clarify that such a mapping from native to common fields would be defined in the dataset that is being aggregated _into_ to avoid the need to modified subdatasets for this purpose (and for a particular purpose that may or may not be what the \"main\" function of any dataset is envisioned to be).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1546874381,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MTk2ODY3OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3107#issuecomment-451968678"},"message":"- I think that the set should be the subset of https://schema.org/Dataset and not introduce new/different fields (e.g `Funding` vs `funder`)\n- `I should clarify that such a mapping from native to common fields would be defined in the dataset that is being aggregated into to avoid the need to modified subdatasets for this purpose`.  I think it should be configurable, as it is already the case for aggregation.  Shouldn't be such harmonization step be a post-processing on aggregation and thus no specific logic on \"when/whereto\" is actually specific to it?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546889153,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MjA1MTYzMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3107#issuecomment-452051631"},"message":"\u003e I think that the set should be the subset of https://schema.org/Dataset and not introduce new/different fields (e.g Funding vs funder)\n\nre field keys: the key names are irrelevant, as long as the `@context` matches the specs. I don't mind sticking with the pre-defined terms in schema.org\n\nre \"subset\": Am I correct to assume that this is an argument against having the concept of an issue tracker and distinguish authors from maintainers? I actually thought that both ideas were quite useful and meaningful.\n\n\u003e I think it should be configurable, as it is already the case for aggregation. Shouldn't be such harmonization step be a post-processing on aggregation and thus no specific logic on \"when/whereto\" is actually specific to it?\n\nThat is probably a misunderstanding. I do consider this a post-processing of aggregated metadata. While it could be applied to alter the actual metadata aggregate, I currently believe this is not a good idea. Still, I think the place to put this information is the aggregation target dataset (it is the one that is under the control of a collection maintainer). Sure thing that a leave dataset can also be an aggregation target.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1546896221,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MjA4Njc0OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3107#issuecomment-452086748"},"message":"\u003e re field keys: the key names are irrelevant, as long as the `@context` matches the specs.\n\nI would argue they are relevant since that is what I should use in search/print query - thus better be consistent with the common dictionary\n\n\u003e re \"subset\": ...\n\ngood point... will need to check may be for another vocabulary with those already available?\n\n\u003e I do consider this a post-processing of aggregated metadata. While it could be applied to alter the actual metadata aggregate, I currently believe this is not a good idea.\n\nwell, not to alter the separate extractor aggregates but the record itself, may be with another field listing which fields were \"harmonized\" from other fields, may be even with the source fields from which they have come.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1549538443,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIxMjM3NjgxMDg="},"added":["metadata"],"removed":[]}]}